WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.160
- This little rock has
come from the bottom

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:03.330
of the Pacific Ocean,

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:05.250
some 14,000 feet down.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.750
Now, it may look like a lump of coal,

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.730
but it's actually dense with cobalt,

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.830
nickel, manganese, and copper,

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.320
some of the key minerals in EV batteries.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.780
The fact that a company has
been to the bottom of the ocean

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.310
and brought this to the surface

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.830
shows just how far we're now willing to go

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.000
in the hunt for the minerals

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.950
that will power our electric vehicles.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.170
See, as demand for electric
vehicles increases,

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:26.760
the need for their critical minerals

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.810
is set to explode

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.790
and dramatically outpace supply.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.700
That's pushed some startups
to pioneer new techniques

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.320
that could fundamentally reshape

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.910
how we find and extract

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.380
the minerals for our batteries.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:38.790
So, what do the different tiers

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:41.412
of EV battery mineral mining look like?

00:00:41.412 --> 00:00:44.430
(calm music)

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:45.660
Let's start with this rock.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.700
It's a test sample that comes from here,

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.610
a huge area 1000 miles
off the coast of Mexico

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.380
called the Clarion Clipperton Zone.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.550
And down there our rock

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.080
isn't exactly a rare find.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.720
There are thousands of
these potato-sized nodules.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:58.650
Holding another one of them

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:01.230
is The Metals Company CEO, Gerard Barron.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.690
- We're very fortunate that our nodules

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.430
actually lay on the ocean floor,

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.920
a little bit like golf
balls on a driving range.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:09.660
We don't have to go and dig, or drill,

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.430
or tunnel to find them,

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.020
they're literally laying there

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.640
waiting for us to pick them up.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.980
- The company's plan is to
send down robots like this

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.150
to collect the nodules

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.430
and bring them up to the
surface for processing.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.080
Now, while companies have wanted

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.000
to mine these fields for decades,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.910
a combination of technological limitations

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.320
and regulatory hurdles

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.050
have prevented anyone from
exploiting the resource.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:32.790
But, that could all change

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:36.330
if and when the UN's
International Seabed Authority

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.210
finalizes its regulations
on seabed mining.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:40.920
So far the governing body has given out

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.260
22 licenses to contractors

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.060
to explore the ocean floor,

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.140
but not to mine it.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.610
- It's a bit of a land grab

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.170
and I think as we move closer

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:51.720
to the final mining code being adopted,

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.140
which we expect no later than 2025,

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.030
countries are awakening to the fact

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:00.360
that this is an enormous economic

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:02.790
and national security opportunity.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:03.900
- [George] But, as with most mining,

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.660
deep-sea mining isn't
without its controversy,

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.610
especially as thousands of new species

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.050
have been discovered down there.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:14.250
Some groups have called for an
outright ban of the practice.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:15.840
Meanwhile, countries like Canada

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.790
and companies like BMW and Volvo

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:19.770
have asked for progress to be paused

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.690
until protection of the marine habitat

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:22.800
can be guaranteed.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.080
The Metals Company says that its operation

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:26.670
will have the lightest impact

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:28.380
on the marine ecosystem.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.940
- Some people wanna move forward

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:33.390
in a very precautionary manner, as do we,

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:36.630
that's why we've spent now
more than 400 million dollars

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.000
to fully understand those impacts.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.890
- Now, while our ocean floor minerals

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.540
have been known about for decades,

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.340
finding new deposits to mine

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.620
can be challenging and time consuming.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.630
It usually involves
setting off seismic charges

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:49.980
or taking core samples

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:52.410
and then spending weeks
analyzing that data.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.350
But, startup Fleet Space says
that its satellite network

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:56.700
could change that.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.590
- We do an ultrasound for Mother Earth.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:02.460
We can't wait 15 years
to find each new deposit,

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.530
as has been the case in mining before.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:05.820
- [George] The company deploys a grid

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.760
of ambient noise tomography
sensors in the ground

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.920
that pick up natural micro-vibrations.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.650
- They listen to the ambient
rumbling of the earth

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.350
and generate a three-dimensional
image of the subsurface

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.450
down to about 2.5 kilometers.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:19.530
- The data from those sensors

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:20.970
is then collected in real time

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:22.650
by a network of satellites,

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:24.420
which send it off for instant processing.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.850
Fleet Space says that
this gives its customers,

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:29.940
who includes the likes of
mining giant Rio Tinto,

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:32.220
the ability to know what
might lie beneath them

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:34.920
within days, as opposed to months.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.510
The company told me that usually

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.630
it can take around
600,000 meters of drilling

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:40.860
to find a new deposit.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.420
- No one really wants to do that

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.640
and governments don't want to allow people

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.310
to just drill at random
until they hit things either.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.350
We don't want to turn the
world into Swiss cheese

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:51.270
looking for the metals
that are gonna sustain

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:52.560
our energy transition.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.360
- But there's one potential
source of these minerals

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.580
that doesn't involve going
to the bottom of the ocean

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.140
or drilling lots of holes.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:03:59.940
It involves utilizing a resource

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.580
that is right in front of you right now,

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.380
recycling old batteries.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:05.280
According to analysts,

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:09.330
there's already around 650,000
tons of battery minerals

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:10.530
in the US alone

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.750
in cars, phones, laptops,

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.400
and other industrial sources.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.230
And startup, Redwood Materials,

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:19.890
formed in 2017 by former
Tesla CTO, JB Straubel,

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.750
has the ambition of
recovering the minerals

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:23.010
stored in those items

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:24.660
when they reach the end of their life,

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:27.060
turning them back into battery materials.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.110
The company says they do this

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:29.400
by discharging the batteries

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:32.310
and dismantling them into
their individual components.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:33.870
Those components are then shredded

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.240
and turned into something
called black mass,

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.340
which goes through
hydro-metallurgic process

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:39.960
to isolate the critical minerals.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:41.370
Redwood says this will enable it

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.310
to extract 30% of the lithium and nickel

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.590
and 100% of the cobalt

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:48.330
needed to made a cathode.

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:49.163
The problem is,

00:04:49.163 --> 00:04:51.480
while there's set to be
lots more EVs on the market

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:52.650
over the coming decade,

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:54.180
it will be several more years

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.220
before the batteries in today's EVs

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:58.230
reach the end of their useful life

00:04:58.230 --> 00:04:59.640
and need to be recycled.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.950
So, right now the company
is mainly recycling

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:04.620
batteries from portable electronic devices

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.780
as well as manufacturing scrap,

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:08.550
the waste product that's created

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:09.990
when making batteries.

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:12.150
That's still enough for
the company to get through

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:15.720
40,000 metric tons of
recyclable material a year,

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:17.370
though it has ambitions to create enough

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:19.110
anode and cathode materials

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:22.740
for five million electric
vehicles by 2030.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:24.840
Now these companies may be
taking different approaches,

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:26.880
but they have something in common,

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:28.170
they're all betting on the future

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.660
where these minerals
are valuable and needed

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:32.430
and their plans have come to fruition.

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:35.430
But, right now, the price of
some of these raw materials

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:36.390
has been falling

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:38.460
as demand for EVs softens slightly.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:39.840
And the more minerals like this

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:41.160
that are discovered and mined,

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.230
the cheaper they could become,

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:46.080
as they go from being something
really rare and precious

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.067
to, well, pretty common.

00:05:48.067 --> 00:05:50.650
(upbeat music)

